    {
      "id": "4ab0281eb704441cf05778f03d4555654395e324",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1201212024,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> I am not sure that we always want to do that, although perhaps sometimes we do. It doesn't make a difference here, but for something like FT_STRINGZPAD blindly changing null padding to replacement characters would be wrong.\n\nStrings in pcapng files are FT_STRING_BUT_WE_ALSO_WORK_AROUND_PEOPLE_WHO_THOUGH_THEY_ARE_FT_STRINGZ, in that the terminating NUL is *NOT* required, but readers should treat an internal NUL as an early string terminator - to quote [section 3.5 \"Options\" of the pcapng spec](https://pcapng.github.io/pcapng/draft-tuexen-opsawg-pcapng.html#name-options):\n\n> If an option's value is a string, the value is not necessarily zero-terminated. Software that reads these files MUST NOT assume that strings are zero-terminated, and MUST treat a zero-value octet as a string terminator.\n\nWe don't require adding a null terminator when writing a pcapng file, but, if there's enough broken software that expects it, maybe we need to do that.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
